{"name":"Phosphorescent Blue","hex":"#11eeee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phosphorescent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the property of emitting light for a period of time after the source of excitation is taken away, e.g., in electrostatic storage tubes and cathode-ray tubes."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A phosphorescent substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phosphorescent"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phosphorescence","blocks":[{"text":"Phosphorescence is a type of photoluminescence related to fluorescence. When exposed to light (radiation) of a shorter threshold wavelength, a phosphorescent substance will glow, absorbing the light and reemitting it at a longer wavelength. Unlike fluorescence, a phosphorescent material does not immediately reemit the radiation it absorbs. Instead, a phosphorescent material absorbs some of the radiation energy and reemits it for a much longer time after the radiation source is removed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Phosphorescence.jpg/330px-Phosphorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorescence","wikidata":"Q192275"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/1280px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Phosphorescent, europium-doped, strontium silicate-aluminate oxide powder under visible light, fluorescing/phosphorescing under long-wave UV light, and persistently phosphorescing in total darkness","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhosphorescent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jablonski diagram of an energy scheme used to explain the difference between fluorescence and phosphorescence. The excitation of molecule A to its singlet excited state (1A*) may, after a short time between absorption and emission (fluorescence lifetime), return immediately to ground state, giving off a photon via fluorescence (decay time). However, sustained excitation is followed by intersystem crossing to the triplet state (3A) that relaxes to the ground state by phosphorescence with much longer decay times.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJablonskiSimple.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/1280px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After an electron absorbs a photon of high energy, it may undergo vibrational relaxations and intersystem crossing to another spin state.  Again the system relaxes vibrationally in the new spin state and eventually emits light by phosphorescence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/1280px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An extremely intense pulse of short-wave UV light in a flashtube produced this blue persistent-phosphorescence in the amorphous, fused silica envelope, lasting as long as 20 minutes after the 3.5 microsecond flash.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electron microscope reveals vacancy defects in a crystalline lattice of molybdenum disulfide. The missing sulfur atoms leave dangling bonds between the molybdenum atoms, creating traps in the empty spaces.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoS2_vacancies.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/120px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/250px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosph